<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Serapeum of Alexandria</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Serapeum of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a> was the great temple of the god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serapis">Serapis</a>, founded under Ptolemy III in the third century BCE and rebuilt in Roman times. It also housed a daughter library of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/library-of-alexandria">Library of Alexandria</a>. In 391, after imperial orders against pagan cults, Christians led by Patriarch Theophilus destroyed it, an event seen as marking the end of public paganism in the city.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/serapeum-of-alexandria.jpg" alt="Serapeum of Alexandria" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Daniel Mayer · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Alexandria_-_Pompey%27s_Pillar_-_view_of_ruins.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
